--TEST--
`Core\Http::allowUrl` answers a `Core\Http\Target` rather than a `string`, and a name that resolves to nothing is refused — `rule:http-server/allow-url-pins-the-address`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The approval succeeds and the answer is a value, not laundered text. That is
// the whole of § 2: a `string` answer would have removed `tainted` and left the
// URL exactly as resolvable as it was, so a second lookup between the check and
// the connection could answer differently. The type is written out here because
// what it *is* is the guarantee — nothing is fetched, and no member of the
// target exposes the address it holds.
Core\Http\Target $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://93.184.216.34/ok");
echo "pinned", "\n";

// A granted name that resolves to no address at all is refused rather than
// approved without one: there is no address to pin, and a target that carried
// none would be a target the client had to re-resolve.
try {
    var $missing = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa.invalid/");
    echo "accepted", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A literal address is the one host a case can approve without a resolver: it
# is public, so § 3's table lets it through, and nothing about the case depends
# on what a name server would have said. The second name is over DNS's 63-octet
# label limit, so every resolver refuses it locally.
[capabilities.net]
connect = [
  "93.184.216.34",
  "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa.invalid",
]
--EXPECT--
pinned
Core\Http::allowUrl could not resolve aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa.invalid, so there is no address to pin
